      "content": "---\nslug: campaign-post-package\ntitle: \"Get Your Nail Brand Featured: The Campaign Post Package\"\nexcerpt: \"Mirellé Inspo's Campaign Post Package gets your nail brand featured with guaranteed, scheduled editorial placement, for a fraction of a traditional PR retainer.\"\ndate: \"2026-07-30\"\ndateModified: \"2026-07-30\"\nauthor: \"Ri Mirellé\"\n\ncanonical: \"https://mirelleinspo.com/business/campaign-post-package\"\ntargetKeyword: \"get your nail brand featured\"\nsecondaryKeywords:\n  - \"nail brand pr\"\n  - \"nail publicity\"\n  - \"nail brand press feature\"\n  - \"sponsored editorial post for nail brands\"\n  - \"nail brand ambassadors\"\n  - \"editorial coverage for beauty brands\"\n  - \"brand feature package nail industry\"\n  - \"press feature vs paid ad nail brand\"\n  - \"how to promote nail business\"\nsearchIntent: \"commercial\"\n\ntldr: \"The Campaign Post Package is how a nail brand gets featured in press on purpose: a fixed set of scheduled editorial posts, purchased directly, guaranteed to run, at a fraction of a traditional PR retainer.\"\nentityType: \"EditorialNailPlatform\"\nspeakable: true\n\ncontact:\n  email: \"contact@mirelleinspo.com\"\n\nservices:\n  - title: \"Product Launch Editorial\"\n    description: \"A scheduled editorial post built around your product drop, timed to run when the launch actually needs visibility, not whenever a pitch happens to land.\"\n  - title: \"Brand Story and Founder Feature\"\n    description: \"The founder story and brand positioning written as editorial content, giving your brand the kind of credibility narrative a paid ad format cannot carry.\"\n  - title: \"Trend Report and Seasonal Campaign Feature\"\n    description: \"Your brand placed inside a trend or seasonal editorial angle that's already built to rank, so the feature keeps working in search long after the season ends.\"\n  - title: \"Sponsored Editorial and Affiliate Placement\"\n    description: \"A structured editorial post with an affiliate or product-listing component, for brands that want the feature to also do direct commercial work.\"\n\nschemaType: \"ProfessionalService\"\npostType: \"client-acquisition\"\npainPoint: \"Nail and beauty brand marketers already know press coverage exists as a category. What they don't have is a middle option: they're stuck choosing between a $15,000-a-month PR retainer with no guaranteed placement, or gifting product to influencers and hoping someone posts. Neither gives them a fixed, purchasable outcome.\"\n\nstatBlock:\n  - stat: \"Consumers who see a company featured editorially in a Tier-1 publication trust that company 3.2x more than consumers who see the same company with a paid advertisement in the same publication (82% said an editorial feature increased trust, vs 24% for a paid ad).\"\n    context: \"This is exactly the trust gap a nail brand is buying when it chooses editorial placement over another paid social ad with the same budget.\"\n  - stat: \"The US nail care market is projected to reach approximately $5.3 billion in 2026, with e-commerce now accounting for over 40% of beauty product sales in the US.\"\n    context: \"The category is large and still growing, and online discovery, where a press feature actually lives and ranks, is now the dominant path to purchase.\"\n  - stat: \"92% of consumers say they trust earned media, recommendations and editorial coverage, above all other forms of advertising.\"\n    context: \"The margin here is not small. It's the core reason a fixed editorial placement outperforms another paid impression for the same spend.\"\n\nfaqSchema:\n  - question: \"Isn't this basically the same as just gifting product to an influencer for free coverage?\"\n    answer: \"No. Gifting is unpaid and unguaranteed; the creator decides if, when, and how they post, if at all. A Campaign Post Package is a purchased, scheduled placement: the brand knows exactly what posts run, when, and on which platform, without depending on a creator's goodwill or algorithm luck.\"\n  - question: \"We can't afford a $15K/month PR agency retainer. Is this actually different, or just a smaller version of the same thing?\"\n    answer: \"It's structurally different, not just cheaper. A retainer pays an agency to pitch on your behalf with no guaranteed outcome, so a brand could pay for months with zero placements. A Campaign Post Package is a fixed, purchased set of posts across weeks or months, so the placement itself is the guarantee, not the pitching effort.\"\n  - question: \"How is this different from just running paid social ads for the same budget?\"\n    answer: \"Format and trust, mainly. Editorial coverage reads as independent content, not an ad, and consumers consistently trust editorial features several times more than paid advertising in the same space. A social ad is instantly recognized as paid; an editorial post is not.\"\n  - question: \"What post types can we actually pick for the campaign?\"\n    answer: \"Product Launch Editorial, Trend Report Feature, Brand Story or Founder Feature, Product Review, Seasonal Campaign, Sponsored Editorial, Market Localisation Post for the Arab market, Product Listing or Affiliate Post, or Ad Placement Post. The client selects which types and how many, across the weeks or months of the package.\"\n  - question: \"Will this actually get found in search, or does it just sit on your site with no real traffic?\"\n    answer: \"The page and posts are built specifically to rank and be found organically, not just published and left. That's the entire design intent behind how Mirellé Inspo structures campaign content, unlike a one-off social post that disappears from feeds within days.\"\n\ngalleryImages: []\n---\n\nEvery nail brand marketer researching press coverage in 2026 already knows the two options on the table. There's the traditional PR retainer, somewhere between fifteen thousand and two hundred thousand dollars a month, paid to an agency whose job is to pitch editors on your behalf with no guarantee any of it lands. And there's gifted influencer PR: free, informal, and entirely dependent on whether the creator you sent product to actually feels like posting that week.\n\nNeither of those is a nail brand press feature you can plan around. Both leave you either overpaying for effort with no outcome, or underpaying for an outcome you can't control.\n\n**Get your nail brand featured** through a structure built for the gap between those two extremes: a fixed, scheduled, purchased editorial placement. Not a pitch. Not a hope. A known quantity.\n\n## The Problem With How Nail Brand PR Actually Works Right Now\n\nSearch \"nail brand PR\" or \"how to promote nail business\" and almost everything that ranks falls into one of two buckets. Beauty Independent's widely cited panel of eleven publicists lays out real, credible PR pricing, but every number in it assumes a traditional retainer relationship with a general beauty focus, nothing nail-specific, and no option to simply buy a placement outright. Everything-PR's 2026 breakdown of beauty PR costs by revenue tier is even more explicit about the ceiling: fifteen thousand to two hundred thousand dollars a month, structurally out of reach for most indie and mid-size nail brands, and again, written for beauty broadly rather than nails.\n\nOn the other end, TikTok is full of PR-package unboxing content and Quora threads where creators talk candidly about gifted product and ambassador arrangements. That content is real and the audience language in it is useful, but none of it answers the actual question a brand marketer has: how do I get proper, structured press coverage as a nail brand, at a price I can actually plan a budget around?\n\nThat's the strategic gap. Nothing currently ranking offers a nail-industry-specific, fixed-scope, multi-post editorial package priced well under a PR retainer, with placement that's guaranteed rather than pitched and hoped for, on a platform that already has real organic traffic. Mirellé Inspo built the Campaign Post Package to sit exactly there.\n\n## What a Campaign Post Package Actually Is\n\nA **brand feature package in the nail industry** works differently from either PR agency or influencer route, and the difference is the point.\n\nWith a retainer agency, you're buying effort. The agency pitches editors, follows up, negotiates, and after a month, you might have a placement, or you might have an invoice and nothing else. With influencer gifting, you're buying a chance. You send product, and the creator decides entirely on their own terms whether, when, and how to post about it, if they post at all.\n\nWith the Campaign Post Package, you're buying the placement itself. The scope is fixed before you pay: a set number of editorial posts, across a defined number of weeks or months, in formats you choose from a specific list. Product Launch Editorial. Trend Report Feature. Brand Story or Founder Feature. Product Review. Seasonal Campaign. Sponsored Editorial. Market Localisation Post for the Arab market. Product Listing or Affiliate Post. Ad Placement Post. You pick what fits your goals for this cycle, and that's what runs. No pitching phase. No dependency on a third party's schedule or mood.\n\nThis is **nail brand publicity** structured the way a brand marketer actually wants to buy media: as a known deliverable, not an ongoing bet.\n\n## Why Editorial Placement Beats Another Paid Ad\n\nIf the budget question is really \"should this money go to a feature or to another round of paid social,\" the research on that question is not close. Consumers who see a company featured editorially in a Tier-1 publication trust that company 3.2 times more than consumers who see the same company running a paid advertisement in the same publication, with 82% saying an editorial feature increased their trust versus 24% for a paid ad. That gap holds specifically across the United States, United Kingdom, Canada, and Australia, the same four markets this package is built for.\n\nNielsen's research on trust in advertising backs the same pattern from a different angle: 92% of consumers say they trust earned media, meaning recommendations and editorial coverage, above every other advertising format. A social ad is recognized instantly as paid the second someone scrolls past it. An editorial post isn't read that way at all, which is exactly why it converts differently.\n\nNone of this is happening in a shrinking category, either. The US nail care market is projected to reach roughly $5.3 billion in 2026, with e-commerce now making up more than 40% of US beauty product sales. Discovery has moved almost entirely online, which means a press feature that's actually built to rank in search does real, ongoing work for a brand long after the day it's published. A paid ad stops the moment the budget runs out. An indexed editorial feature keeps showing up in search results for anyone still typing \"nail brand press feature\" or \"editorial coverage for beauty brands\" months later.\n\n## Press Feature vs. Paid Ad vs. Influencer Gifting: What You're Actually Choosing Between\n\nBrand marketers comparing options at this stage aren't discovering that press coverage exists. They already know the category. What they're weighing is which structure actually delivers a predictable result for the budget in front of them.\n\nA PR retainer buys pitching effort with no guaranteed placement, at a price point most indie and mid-tier nail brands can't sustain month over month. Influencer gifting buys a chance at organic coverage, at no direct cost, but with zero control over whether, when, or how it happens, which is precisely the measurement problem marketers keep running into when they try to justify creator spend without standardized outcomes. A Campaign Post Package buys the placement directly: fixed scope, fixed timeline, guaranteed to run, priced well under a retainer.\n\nIs a sponsored editorial post worth it for a nail brand weighing these three? Given the trust differential between editorial and paid formats, and given that gifting offers no guarantee at all, a purchased editorial placement is the only one of the three that combines a known cost with a known outcome.\n\n## How Many Posts Should a Nail Brand's Press Package Actually Include\n\nThere's no single right number. It depends on the goal for this cycle: a single high-impact Product Launch Editorial timed to a drop, or a longer Seasonal Campaign spread across several posts to sustain visibility through a quarter. What stays constant is that the count is fixed and known before you commit, not an open-ended retainer that could produce one placement or ten depending on how the pitching goes that month.\n\n## A Note on Markets\n\nThis package runs across the United States, United Kingdom, Canada, and Australia, and the credibility claims here are built to hold across all four rather than assume one market's numbers apply everywhere. The $5.3 billion nail care figure and the $15,000-to-$200,000 retainer benchmarks both come from US-focused research and should be read as US-specific, not global. Disclosure norms around sponsored and editorial content differ in enforcement detail between the FTC, the UK's ASA, Canadian Ad Standards, and Australia's AANA, so nothing here treats a single country's rule as a universal standard. What does hold across all four markets is the trust research: the 3.2x credibility study spans exactly this footprint, US, UK, Canada, and Australia, which is why it's the number this page leans on hardest.\n\n## Get Your Nail Brand Featured\n\nMirellé Inspo built the Campaign Post Package because nothing else answers the actual question a nail brand marketer is asking in 2026: not \"does press coverage work,\" but \"how do I buy a guaranteed feature instead of gambling on a pitch or a gift.\" That's what this is. A fixed scope, a fixed price, and a placement that runs because it was purchased, not because someone got lucky with an editor's inbox or an influencer's mood.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Isn't this basically the same as just gifting product to an influencer for free coverage?**\n\nNo. Gifting is unpaid and unguaranteed; the creator decides if, when, and how they post, if at all. A Campaign Post Package is a purchased, scheduled placement: the brand knows exactly what posts run, when, and on which platform, without depending on a creator's goodwill or algorithm luck.\n\n**We can't afford a $15K/month PR agency retainer. Is this actually different, or just a smaller version of the same thing?**\n\nIt's structurally different, not just cheaper. A retainer pays an agency to pitch on your behalf with no guaranteed outcome, so you could pay for months with zero placements. A Campaign Post Package is a fixed, purchased set of posts across weeks or months, so the placement itself is the guarantee, not the pitching effort.\n\n**How is this different from just running paid social ads for the same budget?**\n\nFormat and trust, mainly. Editorial coverage is read as independent content, not an ad, and consumers consistently trust editorial features several times more than paid advertising in the same space. A social ad is instantly recognized as paid; an editorial post is not.\n\n**What post types can we actually pick for the campaign?**\n\nProduct Launch Editorial, Trend Report Feature, Brand Story or Founder Feature, Product Review, Seasonal Campaign, Sponsored Editorial, Market Localisation Post for the Arab market, Product Listing or Affiliate Post, or Ad Placement Post. You select which types and how many, over the weeks or months of the package.\n\n**Will this actually get found in search, or does it just sit on your site with no real traffic?**\n\nThe page and posts are built specifically to rank and be found organically, not just published and left. That's the entire design intent behind how Mirellé Inspo structures campaign content, unlike a one-off social post that disappears from feeds within days."
