      "content": "---\nslug: nail-industry-media-kit\ntitle: \"Nail Industry Media Kit: Advertise Your Beauty Brand on Mirellé Inspo\"\nexcerpt: \"Mirellé Inspo's nail industry media kit gives beauty brands real audience numbers, rate transparency, and direct access to salon owners, nail techs, and nail-obsessed consumers already reading.\"\ndate: \"2026-07-30\"\ndateModified: \"2026-07-30\"\nauthor: \"Ri Mirellé\"\n\ncanonical: \"https://mirelleinspo.com/business/nail-industry-media-kit\"\ntargetKeyword: \"nail industry media kit\"\nsecondaryKeywords:\n  - \"advertise on a nail industry website\"\n  - \"beauty website advertising rates\"\n  - \"banner ad beauty website\"\n  - \"sponsor a nail blog\"\n  - \"nail magazine advertising rates\"\n  - \"display ad beauty industry\"\n  - \"CPM beauty website\"\n  - \"advertise beauty brand online\"\n  - \"nail blog sponsorship\"\n  - \"beauty publication ad rates\"\nsearchIntent: \"commercial\"\n\ntldr: \"A nail industry media kit from Mirellé Inspo gives beauty brands audience data, ad placement formats, and rate card pricing to advertise directly to salon owners, nail techs, and nail-focused consumers instead of a diluted general audience.\"\nentityType: \"EditorialNailPlatform\"\nspeakable: true\n\ncontact:\n  email: \"contact@mirelleinspo.com\"\n\nservices:\n  - title: \"Homepage Banner Placement\"\n    description: \"Premium above-the-fold and homepage banner inventory that puts your brand in front of every visitor before they click into content, sold on a flat-rate or CPM basis depending on flight length.\"\n  - title: \"In-Content Display Advertising\"\n    description: \"Banner and rich-media units placed inside the nail and beauty content salon owners, techs, and consumers are already reading, positioned for engagement rather than passive scroll-past.\"\n  - title: \"Sponsored Editorial Placement\"\n    description: \"A branded feature or sponsored article slot within Mirellé Inspo's editorial lineup, built to read as legitimate coverage rather than an obvious ad unit.\"\n  - title: \"Custom Newsletter Inclusion\"\n    description: \"A dedicated slot in Mirellé Inspo's audience communications, reaching subscribers who opted in specifically for nail-industry content.\"\n\nschemaType: \"ProfessionalService\"\npostType: \"client-acquisition\"\npainPoint: \"Beauty brands trying to advertise online keep landing in the same two bad spots: a self-serve platform like Google or Meta that spreads their budget across a broad, algorithm-guessed audience most of whom will never buy a nail product, or a legacy trade-magazine media kit that hides its rates behind a sales call and sells raw pageviews instead of a buyer-ready audience. Neither gets a nail or beauty brand's message in front of the people actually deciding what goes on their nail cart or their salon shelf.\"\n\nstatBlock:\n  - stat: \"Display ad CPMs on niche and industry websites typically run $3 to $10, with broader web display CPM benchmarks around $2 to $5 for standard placements.\"\n    context: \"Source: WordStream, 'The Comprehensive Guide to Online Advertising Costs for 2026.' Gives a brand marketer a real pricing anchor for judging whether any nail industry media kit rate, including this one, is reasonable.\"\n  - stat: \"Publisher advertising costs for on-site placements typically run from $500 to $20,000 or more per campaign depending on guaranteed impressions, banner size, and flight length, with niche and vertical publishers sitting toward the lower-to-mid end of that range.\"\n    context: \"Source: Improvado, 'Publisher Media Kits: 30 Examples for Digital Advertising.' Places a vertical nail and beauty publisher inside the normal, budget-accessible range of publisher ad buys, not outside it.\"\n  - stat: \"Niche and micro-audience blogs consistently convert better for advertisers with a specific, well-matched product than general-interest blogs, because the message reaches only readers already interested in that exact category.\"\n    context: \"Source: ProBlogger, 'How to Blog: Choose a Niche for Your Blog.' Backs the core case for this page: a small, entirely relevant nail and beauty audience outperforms a larger unfocused one for this category of product.\"\n\nfaqSchema:\n  - question: \"Is it even worth advertising on a niche site like this if the audience is small?\"\n    answer: \"Yes, if the audience is the right one. A smaller audience made entirely of salon owners, nail techs, and nail-obsessed consumers converts better for a nail or beauty brand than a much bigger general audience that isn't specifically interested in nails. Niche-relevant reach beats generic scale for this kind of product.\"\n  - question: \"How much does an on-site ad placement actually cost?\"\n    answer: \"Rates depend on placement type, size, and flight length, similar to how any publisher prices display advertising, typically ranging from a few hundred dollars for a smaller placement to several thousand for premium, high-visibility spots over a longer run. Request the media kit for exact current rates rather than relying on general industry ranges.\"\n  - question: \"What do I actually get for the money, is this just a banner nobody notices?\"\n    answer: \"No. This is a placement on a page actual salon owners, nail techs, and brand-conscious readers are already reading for nail-industry content, not a random ad slot bought through a generic ad network. Placement and format options are laid out clearly in the media kit.\"\n  - question: \"How is this different from just running Google or Instagram ads myself?\"\n    answer: \"Self-serve ad platforms put your brand in front of a broad, algorithm-guessed audience. This placement puts it directly in front of people already reading nail-industry content, a warmer, more relevant audience for a nail or beauty product specifically.\"\n  - question: \"Can I see real numbers before I commit, traffic, audience, past results?\"\n    answer: \"Yes, that's exactly what the media kit is for. Request it and you'll get the audience breakdown, traffic figures, and placement specs up front, before any commitment.\"\n\ngalleryImages: []\n---\n\nAsk a brand marketer where to advertise a nail product and most will say the same two things: run it through Google or Meta and let the algorithm sort it out, or find whatever trade magazine still takes ad calls. Neither answer is wrong, exactly. Both are incomplete.\n\nA **nail industry media kit** solves the actual problem: getting a beauty brand's message in front of an audience that already cares about nails, without paying for the 90% of impressions a broad platform serves to people who will never buy the product. Mirellé Inspo's media kit exists for brand marketers who want that audience, at a real rate, without a sales call standing between them and the numbers.\n\n## The problem with how most beauty brands advertise online\n\nRun the campaign through Google Display Network or Instagram, and the targeting is a guess dressed up as precision. The algorithm reaches for lookalike audiences and interest categories, and a meaningful share of the spend lands on people who have never bought a nail product and never will. That is the tradeoff of scale: a wide net catches everyone, including everyone who does not matter to a nail or beauty brand.\n\nThe other default option is the legacy trade-magazine media kit. NAILS Magazine, the nail industry's long-running trade publication, still offers exactly this: homepage banners, sponsored articles, custom newsletters, sold through a named publisher contact after a call. It works. It is also built the way corporate media kits have been built for twenty years, leaning on raw traffic and pageview counts rather than a buyer-specific case for why this particular audience converts, and it rarely puts a number in front of a marketer without a conversation first.\n\nNeither option is built for a brand marketer who wants to compare real numbers against other niche options on their own schedule, before anyone gets on the phone.\n\n## What advertising on Mirellé Inspo actually gets you\n\nMirellé Inspo's audience is not a diffuse trade-magazine readership. It is salon owners, working nail technicians, and consumers who are already deep enough into nail culture to be reading nail-industry content in the first place, not scrolling past it. That is the entire pitch: **advertise on a nail industry website** where the reader was already interested before the ad loaded.\n\nThe placement formats are built around how brands actually buy, not around what is easiest for a publisher to sell:\n\n**Homepage banner placement.** Above-the-fold inventory that every visitor sees before they click into a single piece of content, priced flat-rate or CPM depending on flight length.\n\n**In-content display advertising.** Banner and rich-media units placed inside the actual articles and guides salon owners and techs are reading, positioned where attention already is instead of competing for it.\n\n**Sponsored editorial placement.** A branded feature that reads like real coverage in the editorial lineup, not an obvious ad unit a reader learns to scroll past.\n\n**Custom newsletter inclusion.** A slot in front of subscribers who opted into nail-industry content specifically, one of the more qualified audiences a beauty brand can reach on any platform.\n\n## What a nail brand advertiser actually pays\n\nStraight answer, not a range that dodges the question: display ad CPMs on niche and industry websites typically run <strong>$3 to $10</strong>, against broader web display benchmarks of $2 to $5 for standard placements, according to WordStream's 2026 advertising cost data. Full publisher campaigns, across all site sizes and formats, typically run from $500 to $20,000 or more depending on guaranteed impressions, banner size, and flight length, per Improvado's review of 30 publisher media kits, with vertical and niche publishers sitting toward the lower-to-mid end of that range rather than the top.\n\nThat puts a nail industry media kit placement in the same normal, budget-accessible category as the household-name publishers a brand marketer might already be evaluating, not in some fringe or inflated tier reserved for niche sites. Request the current rate card for exact numbers. General benchmarks tell you whether a rate is reasonable; they do not replace the actual card.\n\n## Why a smaller, targeted audience beats a bigger generic one\n\nThis is the argument that actually matters, and it holds up outside of any one platform's marketing copy: niche and micro-audience sites consistently convert better for advertisers with a specific, well-matched product than general-interest sites do, because the message reaches only readers already interested in that exact category instead of a diluted general audience, per ProBlogger's guidance on niche publishing.\n\nFor a nail or beauty brand specifically, that means a placement in front of a thousand salon owners and nail techs is worth more than a placement in front of ten thousand people who happen to be browsing a general lifestyle site. **Beauty website advertising rates** should be read against who is actually seeing the ad, not just how many people are seeing it.\n\nDirect buying, negotiating a rate with a specific publisher rather than routing everything through a programmatic exchange, still earns its place for exactly this reason: niche and local audiences where relevance and trust matter more than raw reach. A media kit is how that negotiation starts with real information instead of a guess.\n\n## How the process actually works\n\nRequest the media kit. Review the audience breakdown, traffic figures, and placement inventory it contains. Pick a placement and format that fits the campaign and budget. Confirm flight dates. Go live. No discovery call is required to see a number, and no account setup is required to reserve a spot, though Mirellé Inspo